\section{Introduction}
This is taken from https://www.rfcandy.biz/communication/pa313.html
and it's not quite the same as a normal filter.  The bandwidth where
the impedance matches well is fairly narrow.  And though this is a low
pass filter, what happens on the low side is not really low pass.
It's more of a semi-bandpass filter with uneven and less filtering on
the low side.  But you can define the Q, so you can make a filter
with high Q that's more suitable for a class C amplifier.

Figure \ref{PiFilter} shows a circuit diagram for a Pi filter where R2
is the input resistance and R1 is the output resistance.
\begin{figure}[ht]
  \caption{Pi Filter}\label{PiFilter}
  \begin{center}
  \includesvg[scale=.25]{PiFilter}
  \end{center}
\end{figure}

To calculate the values for a Pi filter, the first thing you need to
calculate is the Q of the filter, which is:
\begin{dmath}
  Q = \frac{f_0}{BW}
\end{dmath}
where $f_0$ is the center frequency of the filter and $BW$ is the
3dB bandwidth of the filter.

To calculate the impedance of C1, use:
\begin{dmath}
  X_{C1} = \frac{R_1}{Q}
\end{dmath}

To calculate the impedance of C2, use:
\begin{dmath}
  X_{C2} = R_2 \sqrt{\frac{\frac{R_1}{R_2}}{(Q^2 + 1) - \frac{R_1}{R_2}}}
\end{dmath}

The impedance of L is:
\begin{dmath}
  X_{L} = \frac{QR_1 + \frac{R_1R_2}{X_{c2}}}{Q^2 + 1}
\end{dmath}

Then convert impedance to capacitor and inductor values using the
standard formulas for that.

As an example, suppose we have a frequency of 435MHz, desired
bandwidth of 100MHz, R2 = 4.63, and R1 = 50

\begin{dmath}
  Q = \frac{435e6}{100e6} = 4.35
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  X_{C1} = \frac{50}{4.35} = 11.49
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  X_{C2} = 4.63 \sqrt{\frac{\frac{50}{4.63}}{(4.35^2 + 1) - \frac{4.63}{50}}} = 5.048
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  X_{L} = \frac{(4.35)(50) + \frac{(4.63)(50)}{5.048}}{4.35^2 + 1} = 13.24
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  C_1 = \frac{1}{2 * \pi * 435e6 * 11.49} = 31.8e-12
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  C_2 = \frac{1}{2 * \pi * 435e6 * 5.048} = 73.2e-12
\end{dmath}
\begin{dmath}
  L = \frac{13.24}{2 * \pi * 435e6} = 4.84e-09
\end{dmath}

Note if using as a PA output filter you must subtract the
collector-emitter (or equivalent) capacitance from C2.

Use the same impedances but reverse the capacitors and inductors to
make a low pass filters.

Pi filters can be chained to make the filters sharper or to do
bandpass or notch filters.  When chaining, you would generally have
the input and output resistances $R_1$ and $R_2$ be the same all the
filters but one, usually 50 ohms.  Then the equation for C2 simplifies
and $X_{C2} = X_{C1}$.  Note that combining filters can cause odd
resonances outside the filtered area in the semi-passband.
